WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:00.968
This is Jake

00:00:00.968 --> 00:00:01.744
Hi.

00:00:01.744 --> 00:00:02.435
I’m Jake.

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:06.000
In February 2020 he posted his first video
to TikTok.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.594
Alright, mom, what are you doing today?

00:00:07.594 --> 00:00:08.450
I’m going to make a beat!

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:12.000
I had my mom make a beat that I had already
pre-made.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.019
And I recorded, I'm like, okay, press these
buttons.

00:00:14.019 --> 00:00:16.443
And we'll make it look like you just made
it in two minutes.

00:00:16.443 --> 00:00:18.060
I need a producer tag

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.269
Okay, what do you want it to be?

00:00:20.269 --> 00:00:21.968
Ayo mama on the beat.

00:00:21.968 --> 00:00:24.730
Within hours the video was racking up millions
of views.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:27.582
So, Jake made a few more videos featuring
his mom,

00:00:27.582 --> 00:00:29.180
but with one small tweak.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.130
My brother had the idea, he was like,

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:35.520
yo, what if we like put a TikTok like in front
of the phone and we filmed it...

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.520
Hey mom.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:37.520
Have you ever heard this song before?

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.180
The format was simple but clever:

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.810
Jake would mashup songs on TikTok that were
already going viral.

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:42.810
[overlapping] Hey mom.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.314
Have you ever heard this song before?

00:00:44.314 --> 00:00:45.014
It worked.

00:00:45.014 --> 00:00:47.160
With each post, he was racking up millions
more views.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.555
But he didn’t stop there.

00:00:48.555 --> 00:00:51.797
And we were like, we need to put my vocals
on the end of it.

00:00:51.797 --> 00:00:56.390
And if the song goes viral, then like we can
drop it ourselves and become an artist.

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:59.110
Hood baby part 2, featuring yours truly.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:01.370
Up down right down...

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:04.857
We're just going to name myself, JVKE
and we're going to go at it.

00:01:04.857 --> 00:01:06.958
Ayo mommas gonna make this a bop.

00:01:06.958 --> 00:01:10.503
“Up down right down looking for your love
right now.”

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:13.680
It just so happens that the first one that
I put my vocals on

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.369
went absolutely bananas viral.

00:01:19.228 --> 00:01:22.970
Jake’s 15 second snippet of a song was taking
over TikTok.

00:01:23.755 --> 00:01:28.144
Once it was at 1 million videos, 
Charli D’Amelio used it.

00:01:28.144 --> 00:01:29.817
That means that this is about to be insane.

00:01:29.817 --> 00:01:31.688
Like it's not ending at a million.

00:01:31.688 --> 00:01:33.124
It's starting at a million.

00:01:33.124 --> 00:01:34.870
At the time, Jake didn’t even have a manager.

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:37.050
But he knew he needed one fast.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.023
We have these fifteen seconds,
we have labels calling us...

00:01:40.023 --> 00:01:40.996
What do we do?

00:01:40.996 --> 00:01:43.840
That was actually when I got on the phone
with my now manager

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.772
and he was like “Call me, right now!”

00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:49.118
He was like, "Hey, can you finish this song
in 24 hours?"

00:01:49.118 --> 00:01:51.843
Clearing the “Hoody Baby” sample ended
up taking a few days.

00:01:51.843 --> 00:01:55.096
By the time we got it out, it was at 5 million
videos.

00:01:55.096 --> 00:01:57.420
It ended up going to 15 million.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.011
If you compound all the views 
of all the 15 million videos,

00:02:01.011 --> 00:02:04.459
it's like over 9 Billion views.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.849
This is just the beginning of Jake’s story.

00:02:08.849 --> 00:02:11.682
But what makes it so interesting isn't how
dramatic it is,

00:02:11.682 --> 00:02:15.960
but how often stories just like his occurred
in 2020.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.000
Over the last few months I’ve been working
with The Pudding

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.330
to put experiences like Jake’s into perspective.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:25.560
We dug into the data and stories behind dozens
of emerging artists’ TikTok hits.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:30.010
To figure out what internet fame actually
means for new music artists.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:35.218
It turns out, this is way more than a story
about algorithms or going viral.

00:02:35.218 --> 00:02:38.272
It’s a story about the longstanding 
tug-of-war between

00:02:38.272 --> 00:02:42.216
artists, platforms, and music industry giants.

00:02:42.216 --> 00:02:44.586
You might be surprised who’s winning.

00:02:49.868 --> 00:02:53.199
If I were to review this project, I'd probably
never do it again.

00:02:53.199 --> 00:02:56.050
That is Matt Daniels, a journalist 
at The Pudding

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:59.000
where he digs into culture stories 
through data.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.650
In the Fall of 2021, we started a project that
turned into what Matt describes as...

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:06.560
One of the hardest data projects I've ever
worked on.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:08.016
It seemed simple on paper:

00:03:08.016 --> 00:03:12.915
create a data set of Indie and DIY artist
that went viral on TikTok in 2020

00:03:12.915 --> 00:03:17.339
and determine if that virality was enough
to change the outlook of their career.

00:03:17.339 --> 00:03:21.473
Because it really was their first 
major, major exposure

00:03:21.473 --> 00:03:23.260
to a huge fan base on the internet.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:26.140
But, there were a few big problems 
right from the start.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.860
The biggest was there wasn’t 
a useful data set

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:32.090
of the most popular songs on TikTok from 2020.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:35.150
We banged our head against the wall

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:38.569
trying to figure out “how do we reconstruct
charts from TikTok?".

00:03:38.569 --> 00:03:43.219
It turns out, there’s a lot of playlists
on Spotify that compile viral TikTok hits.

00:03:43.219 --> 00:03:45.130
And there’s also this tool called Chartmetric.

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:48.500
Which among other things captures historical
data of those playlists

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:51.840
and tracks what songs have been added and
subtracted to them and when.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.090
So, we got to work.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:55.605
And pulled all of the songs from as many playlists
as we could find

00:03:55.605 --> 00:03:58.993
that were added between January 
and December 2020.

00:03:58.993 --> 00:04:02.000
Then we ranked the songs by their popularity
on TikTok,

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.190
filtering out any that got fewer than 
100,000 posts.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:09.639
Which brought us down to about 1500 songs
that went viral in 2020.

00:04:09.639 --> 00:04:12.080
The biggest challenge I would say is once
we had our arms

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.354
wrapped around these 1500 songs

00:04:14.354 --> 00:04:16.849
making the decision of, is this an established
artist

00:04:16.849 --> 00:04:18.449
that had a TikTok hit

00:04:18.449 --> 00:04:20.762
or is this the artist's big break?

00:04:20.762 --> 00:04:24.359
And a big break is a very subjective decision.

00:04:24.359 --> 00:04:28.539
A lot of the artists in this list were obviously
very established.

00:04:28.539 --> 00:04:31.569
Cardi B going viral on TikTok is not particularly
impressive

00:04:31.569 --> 00:04:34.316
versus somebody who has never released a song
before.

00:04:34.316 --> 00:04:36.919
So we went back to Chartmetric to dig into
more data points

00:04:36.919 --> 00:04:39.405
behind these songs and the artists that made
them.

00:04:39.405 --> 00:04:43.465
Including their Spotify monthly listeners,
the number of times they've been playlisted,

00:04:43.465 --> 00:04:45.305
the number of tracks they've released.

00:04:45.305 --> 00:04:50.529
Which made it a lot easier to decide: did this
artist have a career beforehand?

00:04:50.529 --> 00:04:53.599
Eventually, after filtering out all of the
established artists

00:04:53.599 --> 00:04:58.689
we narrowed our list to a sample of 125 artists
we felt hit all the marks.

00:04:58.689 --> 00:05:00.900
They all went viral on TikTok in 2020

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.884
and as far as we can determine, 
it was their big break.

00:05:03.884 --> 00:05:06.000
Can I also say something really quickly...

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:06.872
Yeah yeah.

00:05:06.872 --> 00:05:10.402
It doesn't actually matter how many artists
we examined.

00:05:10.402 --> 00:05:14.050
There's probably thousands of new artists
that went viral on TikTok.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:19.819
So what we wanted to do was just wrap our
arms around a cohort of artists that are experiencing

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:23.789
this phenomenon and, and then say what, what
happened to them afterwards?

00:05:23.789 --> 00:05:27.959
What happened to these artists' after they
went viral was eye-opening.

00:05:27.959 --> 00:05:31.690
And nearly everyone of those stories starts
with Spotify.

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:36.449
The speed and the intensity with which TikTok
sends things viral.

00:05:36.449 --> 00:05:37.449
It's crazy.

00:05:37.449 --> 00:05:39.511
That’s Elias Leight: he’s a music journalist

00:05:39.511 --> 00:05:43.710
and his reporting on TikTok and the music
industry is extensive.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.999
The virality itself is not necessarily new

00:05:45.999 --> 00:05:49.080
but TikTok is basically just like 
a machine gun

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:50.650
shooting out viral songs

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.830
like even more than daily, honestly.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:55.919
The two big differences are how many viral
moments it creates

00:05:55.919 --> 00:06:00.830
and then how directly that virality correlates
with streaming increase

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:04.009
which is why the labels are so 
obsessed with it.

00:06:04.009 --> 00:06:06.930
You can see how that played out in real time
by looking at this chart

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:11.389
which captures the explosive virality of JVKE’s
track “Upside Down” on TikTok.

00:06:11.389 --> 00:06:14.589
But what it doesn’t show is that while Jake’s
track was going viral

00:06:14.589 --> 00:06:17.669
people were flocking to Spotify 
to stream it.

00:06:17.669 --> 00:06:20.720
This is the TikTok-to-Spotify pipeline.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:22.860
I remember when we first released the song

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:26.422
it was just like a bottle rocket,
up to like 500,000 streams a day.

00:06:26.422 --> 00:06:27.949
I was like, what is happening?

00:06:27.949 --> 00:06:30.789
I didn’t even know really what that meant
at the time.

00:06:30.789 --> 00:06:34.029
What is meant is that the track was going
to get playlisted.

00:06:34.029 --> 00:06:36.779
In fact within a month of “Upside Down’s”
official release

00:06:36.779 --> 00:06:40.740
it was on 98 editorial playlists: including
"New Music Friday"

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.669
which has almost 4 million followers.

00:06:42.669 --> 00:06:45.629
It landed on the "Global Viral 50" Spotify
Charts.

00:06:45.629 --> 00:06:49.860
And as a result, JVKE went from zero monthly
listeners on August 18th

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:52.781
to 3.4 million by October.

00:06:52.781 --> 00:06:56.819
As I’m editing this piece, he has 
over 8 million.

00:06:56.819 --> 00:07:00.240
This pipeline from TikTok to Spotify wasn’t
unique to JVKE.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:04.480
It happened to nearly every one of the 125
artists on our list.

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:06.699
So, I feel like TikTok is one of the main
platforms

00:07:06.699 --> 00:07:10.969
where people actually leave the app to go
and add music to their library.

00:07:10.969 --> 00:07:13.360
That’s L.Dre - who’s song Steven Universe
has been used

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:16.297
in more than 10 million video posts.

00:07:16.297 --> 00:07:18.528
Whenever one of my videos went viral

00:07:18.528 --> 00:07:22.006
most of the comments were people begging me
to release it.

00:07:22.006 --> 00:07:23.759
That's kind of the culture on TikTok.

00:07:23.759 --> 00:07:26.979
When they hear something, they really wanna
go listen to it.

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:31.199
I pretty much went from having just like a
few thousand monthly listeners

00:07:31.199 --> 00:07:35.509
to just a rapid incline for like a year or
two straight

00:07:35.509 --> 00:07:37.550
where it was just steady going up.

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:38.759
And what was more exciting to see

00:07:38.759 --> 00:07:41.369
was that almost all of the artists, including
L.Dre

00:07:41.369 --> 00:07:44.619
had some of their other tracks get editorially
playlisted.

00:07:44.619 --> 00:07:46.539
That's really important because it means that

00:07:46.539 --> 00:07:48.699
once you're on a playlist that Spotify curates,

00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:50.050
it's getting a huge audience

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:54.899
it's a huge signal that the music you're releasing
is going to get streams.

00:07:54.899 --> 00:07:58.059
To get a better sense of how the TikTok-to-Spotify
pipeline worked

00:07:58.059 --> 00:08:01.684
Matt and I analyzed another chart:
The Spotify 200.

00:08:01.684 --> 00:08:05.439
It’s a global chart that shows the top 200
songs on the app every day.

00:08:05.439 --> 00:08:07.540
These songs are stream kings.

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:10.787
So, in a new spreadsheet, we pulled all the
artists whose songs made

00:08:10.787 --> 00:08:14.097
the U.S. Spotify 200 after January 2020

00:08:14.097 --> 00:08:17.099
and filtered out all of the artists who had
well established careers

00:08:17.099 --> 00:08:19.128
or had already charted before then.

00:08:19.128 --> 00:08:21.736
This left us with a new data set to analyze.

00:08:21.736 --> 00:08:26.008
These 332 emerging artists who landed on the
chart for the first time.

00:08:26.008 --> 00:08:30.889
Out of this group, a quarter of them
have TikTok to thank for their big break.

00:08:30.889 --> 00:08:35.750
This is incredibly eye-opening in terms of
TikTok’s influence on the charts.

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:38.960
In terms of what is getting listened to in
music culture.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.688
But let’s backtrack a second.

00:08:40.688 --> 00:08:43.160
Because it’s not just about racking up millions
of streams.

00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:45.696
It’s about how fast 
you’re able to do it.

00:08:45.696 --> 00:08:49.422
And it’s not just about how virality influences
music culture.

00:08:49.422 --> 00:08:52.560
It’s about how it influences music business.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.788
Spotify basically pays out labels according
to

00:08:56.788 --> 00:09:00.536
their share of the total streams in a given
time period.

00:09:00.536 --> 00:09:03.520
If you get a really big hit that, you know,

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:06.270
does a billion streams over six months or
something

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:09.210
that can add a few points to your market share

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:12.292
which then increases your payout of the Spotify
pool.

00:09:12.292 --> 00:09:16.610
It's really like a ruthless competition for
this market share number

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:19.130
that no listener cares about at all

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:21.590
but the record labels watch obsessively.

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:25.530
Despite this obsession, look what’s happened
over the past 4 years.

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:29.140
Major labels have slowly conceded their total
Spotify market share

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:32.000
to independent and self-released artists.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:36.277
To get back a bigger piece of the pie, labels
developed a strategy:

00:09:36.277 --> 00:09:39.880
Monitor TikTok like a hawk and aggressively
try to sign artists

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:41.570
that are rising to the top.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:43.340
In a way, TikTok is great for the labels.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:45.940
They basically sit on top of it, watch everything
come up

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:47.570
and if they get it at the right time

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:50.990
they can probably make their money back on
pretty much one track.

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:54.940
The intensity of these bidding wars 
around viral songs...

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:55.940
It's pretty wild.

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:59.482
It's just like a flat-out sprint to grab the
next viral thing.

00:10:01.525 --> 00:10:03.502
Here's a headline I've seen everywhere. 

00:10:03.502 --> 00:10:06.240
And for legal reasons, I made 
a generic version of it.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:11.002
“Artist with viral TikTok song inks million
dollar record deal with major label.”

00:10:11.002 --> 00:10:12.220
Let's break it down.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:16.070
So you're an artist, you have a song and it’s
doing really, really well

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:18.400
and all these record labels 
are hitting you up.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:20.091
They're like, I want to sign you.

00:10:20.091 --> 00:10:22.440
How can we be a part of this conversation?

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:26.250
So then they will entice you with money, which
is an advance.

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:27.910
So this million dollars, right here?

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:29.130
That’s the advance.

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:31.942
The more virality you have the more zeros
you’ll see.

00:10:31.942 --> 00:10:33.481
This is Mary Rahmani.

00:10:33.481 --> 00:10:35.376
She’s a former TikTok music exec

00:10:35.376 --> 00:10:37.591
who now runs her own label 
with Republic records 

00:10:37.591 --> 00:10:39.070
called Moon Projects.

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:43.900
When I worked at major labels, but under an
imprint that was a little more indie

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:47.550
I would go in between like 50 to a hundred 
thousand dollars for an advance.

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:50.830
And yeah, the major labels are a double triple
that sometimes.

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:55.250
The label signs you, they give you a fat advance
of about a hundred thousand dollars or something

00:10:55.250 --> 00:11:00.431
like that, in exchange for full ownership
of all of your masters

00:11:00.431 --> 00:11:01.719
all of your recorded music.

00:11:01.719 --> 00:11:03.076
So, for that million dollars

00:11:03.076 --> 00:11:07.246
the label now owns the rights to your 
viral TikTok song forever and...

00:11:07.246 --> 00:11:12.470
They would keep around 85% of the royalties
that came in.

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:17.311
You only got your 15% if you recouped the
cost of your advance.

00:11:17.311 --> 00:11:19.060
By the way, that’s Ari Herstand.

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:23.620
I'm the author of "How To Make it in the New
Music Business" and I'm an independent musician.

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:29.000
So right after you ink your million dollar
deal you see a nice $50,000 check from Spotify

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.751
because your song is still riding
that viral wave.

00:11:31.751 --> 00:11:38.283
$42,500 of that check would go to the label,
only $7,500 would go to you…

00:11:38.283 --> 00:11:42.510
but it wouldn’t stay with you for long because
you have to use that check to start paying

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:44.682
back your million dollar advance.

00:11:44.682 --> 00:11:47.151
In essence, an advance is a loan.

00:11:47.151 --> 00:11:51.308
And if you’re only making 15% of the revenue
generated by your song

00:11:51.308 --> 00:11:54.298
it might take a very long time to pay it back.

00:11:54.298 --> 00:11:57.854
So you just have to hope you’re very smart
about the way you use your advance

00:11:57.854 --> 00:12:01.940
or you're extremely successful and your album 
generates millions and millions of dollars.

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:04.470
And then you start earning royalties on the
back end.

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:09.740
The whole approach is basically like initially
you're going to be, you know, 500K, a million,

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:13.400
1.5 million in the hole, and you just have
to dig yourself out of it.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:17.524
This is what a standard major label record
deal has looked like for decades.

00:12:17.524 --> 00:12:21.219
But recently, this part of the headline has
started changing the equation.

00:12:21.219 --> 00:12:25.955
If you have a viral hit, and have proven you
can build a following all on your own.

00:12:25.955 --> 00:12:28.550
Congratulations, you have leverage.

00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:33.680
I mean, if you have a viral hit, probably
you’re getting a lot of offers simultaneously

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:36.146
because labels scrutinize TikTok so closely

00:12:36.146 --> 00:12:38.525
 and they want to be part of these viral things so badly

00:12:38.525 --> 00:12:39.770
Because of that...

00:12:39.770 --> 00:12:42.887
There's been a bigger shift in the last couple
of years than there has been in the last

00:12:42.887 --> 00:12:46.264
50 years in the types of deals

00:12:46.264 --> 00:12:52.450
that labels are starting to offer 
the artists in the artists favor.

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:56.540
Where the label comes to the artist and says,
I know, historically we would take 85%, but

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:59.840
we're not going to do that for you because
you're so valuable.

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:04.403
And you've obviously proven that you can create
a career all on your own.

00:13:04.403 --> 00:13:08.141
So, how about 50/50 we're partners now?

00:13:08.141 --> 00:13:09.930
And you know, we're not going to own your
stuff.

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:11.850
We're just going to do a licensing deal.

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:17.600
Meaning give us the rights to your record
for the next 12 to 15 years.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:20.599
You can still do whatever you want with it,
we'll do whatever we can

00:13:20.599 --> 00:13:22.279
to make more money on this.

00:13:22.279 --> 00:13:24.000
And we'll split it 50/50.

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:29.380
That has never really happened before with
self-released DIY independent artists.

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:33.059
So, how many record deals are actually happening?

00:13:33.059 --> 00:13:36.000
Matt and I decided to tackle this question
from two angles.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:40.081
First by compiling a list of 
around 367 emerging artists

00:13:40.081 --> 00:13:43.990
who landed their first major label 
deal after January 2020.

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:49.339
And then from there, we went row by row, artist
by artist, trying to determine,

00:13:49.339 --> 00:13:52.677
did this person have a viral moment on TikTok?

00:13:52.677 --> 00:13:58.350
And if so, was that cited as one of the reasons
for them getting signed?

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:03.380
It turns out, roughly a third of these deals
happened because an artist's song went viral

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:04.940
on TikTok.

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:08.922
And when Matt and I went back and looked at
our original group of 125 artists,

00:14:08.922 --> 00:14:12.203
we figured out that 46% of them went from unsigned

00:14:12.203 --> 00:14:14.383
to landing a major record label deal. 

00:14:14.383 --> 00:14:19.170
Because when these artists do have leverage,
signing to a major has its benefits.

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:22.881
For one, they have direct relationships with
streaming services like Spotify

00:14:22.881 --> 00:14:26.313
which helps them get more placements on editorial playlists.

00:14:26.313 --> 00:14:31.690
They are also massive international conglomerates,
so they will tell you that they can push you

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:36.123
in Sweden and France and Germany and Japan
at the same time as they push you in America.

00:14:36.123 --> 00:14:39.817
At the end of the day, the biggest difference
is that they're massive banks

00:14:39.817 --> 00:14:42.000
and they can write huge checks. 

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:46.573
But TikTok, has increased the chances for
DIY artists to go viral one day and wake up

00:14:46.573 --> 00:14:51.360
the next morning with a million streams on
Spotify without spending millions of dollars

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:54.960
to record an album, and needing a global team
to promote it.

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:57.580
This is honestly the subject of a lot of debate
right now.

00:14:57.580 --> 00:15:01.269
If you're able to build a lot of leverage
on your own, like how much benefit does

00:15:01.269 --> 00:15:05.400
a big label offer you and what should 
you give up?

00:15:07.987 --> 00:15:11.703
When I looked at the data, I wasn’t that
surprised to all of these of these artists

00:15:11.703 --> 00:15:13.987
signing deals with major labels.

00:15:13.987 --> 00:15:17.548
What I found more compelling was the group
who were likely offered deals

00:15:17.548 --> 00:15:20.653
and decided to continue on their own,
at least for now.

00:15:20.653 --> 00:15:23.570
It's like, I can promote my music.

00:15:23.570 --> 00:15:27.641
I don't have to rely on you to 
make stuff happen for me.

00:15:27.641 --> 00:15:32.274
The leverage is kind of slowly being
put back into the hands of the artists

00:15:32.274 --> 00:15:34.410
and it’s a beautiful thing to see.

00:15:35.064 --> 00:15:38.289
When I was browsing our list of 
125 viral artists

00:15:38.289 --> 00:15:40.611
one name happened to grab my attention: 

00:15:40.611 --> 00:15:42.348
Edith Whiskers.

00:15:42.348 --> 00:15:46.610
It turns out that name is a pseudonym for
the prolific singer songwriter Tom Rosenthal.

00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:55.870
I write fairly boring singer songwriting music
that has gone a bit viral on TikTok.

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:58.266
Tom’s first viral song was his cover of
“Home”

00:15:58.266 --> 00:16:00.977
by Edward Sharp and the Magnetic Zeroes.

00:16:00.977 --> 00:16:04.340
It’s been used in 1.6 million TikTok videos.

00:16:04.340 --> 00:16:09.430
So after the initial TikTok waves hit me,
I thought,

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:12.000
okay, I'm going to release it, but then it
suddenly dawned on me that

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.894
if I released it via my own name,

00:16:14.894 --> 00:16:19.656
I thought this cover will be on the top 
of my lists forever.

00:16:19.656 --> 00:16:23.860
I couldn’t bear the idea of writing all
these original songs and then having one cover

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:26.020
just sitting there at the top.

00:16:26.020 --> 00:16:27.870
So he did the only sensible thing.

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:31.643
I came up with four names, put all those names
on Twitter,

00:16:31.643 --> 00:16:35.190
did a Twitter poll and Edith Whiskers was
the favorite.

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:38.250
So he released the track on Spotify under
the name Edith Whiskers

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:41.170
And it shot straight up to 
the Spotify Viral 50.

00:16:41.170 --> 00:16:44.300
Basically, If you can name a record label
they reached out.

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:47.060
The thing is, Tom is staunchly independent.

00:16:47.060 --> 00:16:49.860
And has some pretty strong feelings about
major labels.

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:54.670
I'm their worst nightmare, really, because
I'm older than, you know, 19.

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:58.230
When record labels said to me “Oh, come
and sign with us because you know, these things

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:02.860
can drop off and we help it grow and flourish
in its own special way.”

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:08.539
Well, I go, “Hang on, I've literally got
the data from the first one over a year now,

00:17:08.539 --> 00:17:12.289
showing that it basically does roughly the
same thing every single day of his life.”

00:17:12.289 --> 00:17:16.990
I just basically said, this is around the kind
of money that it would take to get me interested,

00:17:16.990 --> 00:17:19.779
because I know this is the money that I will
make from these songs.

00:17:19.779 --> 00:17:24.039
That knowledge is really important, especially
if you're independent.

00:17:24.039 --> 00:17:29.495
In 2021, just a few months after going viral,
Tom started his own record label.

00:17:29.495 --> 00:17:30.980
I thought, let's try it.

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:32.720
Let's try and create a fair system.

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:35.684
Let’s actually have a completely 
big rethink about

00:17:35.684 --> 00:17:38.649
how I approach it compared to 
traditional record labels.

00:17:38.649 --> 00:17:42.738
The first thing he nixed was the long tradition
of advance and recoupment.

00:17:42.738 --> 00:17:47.220
Obviously not go crazy and invest lots of
money, but actually invest, you know, one

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:51.530
or 2000 pounds or dollars in someone and say,
look, let's make a few songs,

00:17:51.530 --> 00:17:53.830
but I'm not looking for that money back.

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:56.824
So that's the key difference, I think, is
you’re just investing in people,

00:17:56.824 --> 00:17:59.820
rather than it being this odd loan system.

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:04.700
I want every artist of mine, on my record
label, to understand money.

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:08.564
And the only way they do that is by seeing
money come in straight away

00:18:08.564 --> 00:18:10.868
and understanding how that works.

00:18:10.868 --> 00:18:16.100
It has never been a better time to be a DIY
artist, but that doesn’t mean it’s easy.

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:20.059
I’m at the whims of these almighty algorithms
at all times.

00:18:20.059 --> 00:18:24.320
Like they pretty much decide, you know, whether
I'm gonna eat dinner or whatever.

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:27.350
A few years ago I started following L.Dre
on Instagram

00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:30.000
way before TikTok was even on my radar

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.976
and it seemed like he always had content at
the ready.

00:18:32.976 --> 00:18:37.934
I just need to make that I’m posting stuff,
pretty consistently on any platform I can.

00:18:37.934 --> 00:18:42.580
The fact of the matter is, if you wanna make
it today, you either have to have money to

00:18:42.580 --> 00:18:46.610
hire people, or you just need to also become
a video editor.

00:18:46.610 --> 00:18:48.659
Also become a graphic designer.

00:18:48.659 --> 00:18:51.980
You definitely have to wear a lot of hats
for sure.

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:57.809
Pretty much everyone I talked to said, if
you want exposure, you have to be on TikTok,

00:18:57.809 --> 00:19:01.369
and not only that, you have post post post.

00:19:01.369 --> 00:19:04.092
We recommended posting three to five times
a week

00:19:04.092 --> 00:19:06.351
and now it's three to five times a day,

00:19:06.351 --> 00:19:10.112
Because theres people out there who are willing
to put in the extra work,

00:19:10.112 --> 00:19:11.899
and you’re competing with them now.

00:19:11.899 --> 00:19:15.546
Even if you’re an influencer full time,
it’s a lot of work.

00:19:15.546 --> 00:19:21.678
But it's just because there's so much content
coming in and music is one vertical of many.

00:19:21.678 --> 00:19:25.910
If you’re looking to break as a new artist,
that is kind of the requirement.

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:28.841
The more you post, the more you'll get discovered.

00:19:28.841 --> 00:19:32.779
Out of all of the big existential questions
this project hit me with,

00:19:32.779 --> 00:19:36.369
the one that I can’t stop thinking about
is this:

00:19:36.369 --> 00:19:38.693
Is music just content now?

00:19:38.693 --> 00:19:41.149
Are musicians just content creators?

00:19:41.149 --> 00:19:44.800
I don't know what the actual meaning 
of this was

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:47.420
but like the video killed the radio star thing. 

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:55.164
There's more to the equation than just your
voice on radio.

00:19:55.164 --> 00:19:58.889
And I think that's kind of what you're getting
at here is that there's just so much around

00:19:58.889 --> 00:20:00.360
your ability to create content.

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:03.928
On one hand, if you're signed to a label,
they can help you with that.

00:20:03.928 --> 00:20:09.718
But on the other hand, you're signed to a
label because you're good at that already.

00:20:09.718 --> 00:20:16.899
Matt and I spent months poking at prodding
at the digital footprint of these 125 artists.

00:20:16.899 --> 00:20:21.690
Examining whether or not they would be deemed
successful based on how quickly their TikTok

00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:23.648
and Instagram followers increased,

00:20:23.648 --> 00:20:28.395
or whether they gained youtube subscribers, 
and spotify monthly listeners.

00:20:28.395 --> 00:20:32.492
But one metric that’s just as valuable is
touring.

00:20:32.492 --> 00:20:33.819
People love you on TikTok.

00:20:33.819 --> 00:20:34.547
That's great.

00:20:34.547 --> 00:20:36.010
Will they pay for a show?

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:37.299
You know, can you start selling merch?

00:20:37.299 --> 00:20:40.102
Like, is there a deeper attachment here?

00:20:40.102 --> 00:20:44.020
First we looked at, of these artists, how
many were touring beforehand?

00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:45.988
Unsurprisingly, some of them were.

00:20:45.988 --> 00:20:49.279
Among the artists who had never toured before
or played a show

00:20:49.279 --> 00:20:52.946
about a third of them have had 
at least one show

00:20:52.946 --> 00:20:57.519
and about 15% of them are actually playing festivals now.

00:20:57.519 --> 00:21:03.395
A decent chunk of these artists have been
able to channel their TikTok success into

00:21:03.395 --> 00:21:09.950
performing live music, which is often the
source of financial security for an artist.

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:13.878
You want people to come to your shows and
you want people to buy your albums and your

00:21:13.878 --> 00:21:18.813
t-shirts and be invested in what you're doing
as an all-rounder.

00:21:18.813 --> 00:21:22.087
And actually a viral song is obviously great,

00:21:22.087 --> 00:21:25.590
but it’s about doing something so much stronger than that.

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:31.243
In November 2021, a little over a year after
Jake went viral with his mom on TikTok, he

00:21:31.243 --> 00:21:33.539
played his very first live show.

00:21:33.539 --> 00:21:38.619
"When you perform for the very first time
after everyone said you were just a TikToker."

00:21:43.329 --> 00:21:46.253
That first moment when I was walking onto
the stage

00:21:46.253 --> 00:21:50.189
I was walking kind of slow just to like take
in what was actually happening.

00:21:58.304 --> 00:22:03.579
As I sang the first lyrics to the song and
everyone was just screaming back at me.

00:22:03.579 --> 00:22:05.432
Like I almost was like speechless.

00:22:12.757 --> 00:22:14.423
That target, we were trying to hit of like

00:22:14.423 --> 00:22:17.609
let's convert this virality into 
something real,

00:22:17.609 --> 00:22:19.919
like we hit that target spot on. 

